acing, matching in tournaments, card games or strategies for sport bets [4,3,8,5,1,12]. The Bradley-Terry model is part of a broader class of models that that describe statistical rankings. Specifically, the Bradley-Terry model is a margin of the Plackett-Luce model, see [19] for algebraic descriptions of these models. ...\n> \n> Reference:\n> \n> The semi-algebraic geometry of optimal designs for the Bradley-Terry model\n\nRanking whist players\n\n  * Citing Article\n  * June 2004\n\nDiscrete Mathematics\n\nDavid R. Berman\n\n·\n\nSandra C. McLaurin\n\n·\n\nDouglas D. Smith\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComplete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/258082408_Complete_Mixed_Doubles_Round_Robin_Tournaments)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Berman ... We present a new type of tournament design that we call a complete mixed doubles round robin tournament, CMDRR(n,k), that generalizes spouse-avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments and strict Mitchell mixed doubles round robin tournaments. ... Balanced equitable mixed doubles round-robin tournaments ... Smith, Mitchell tournaments, Bull.\n\n# Complete Mixed Doubles Round Robin Tournaments\n\n  * October 2013\n\n  * Source\n  * arXiv\n\nAuthors:\n\nDavid R. Berman\n\nDavid R. Berman\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nIan N. Wakeling\n\nIan N. Wakeling\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe present a new type of tournament design that we call a complete mixed doubles round robin tournament, CMDRR(n,k), that generalizes spouse-avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments and strict Mitchell mixed doubles round robin tournaments. We show that CMDRR(n,k) exist for all allowed values of n and k apart from 4 exceptions and 31 possible exceptions. We show that a fully resolvable CMDRR(2n,0) exists for all n $\\ge$ 5 and a fully resolvable CMDRR(3n,n) exists for all n $\\ge$ 5 and n odd. We prove a product theorem for constructing CMDRR(n,k).\n\n## Citations (0)\n\n## References (15)\n\nResearchGate has not been able to resolve any citations for this publication.\n\nIncomplete self-orthogonal latin squares\n\nArticle\n\nFull-text available\n\n  * Apr 2009\n\n  * Katherine Heinrich\n  * Lie Zhu\n\nWe show that for all n ≥ 3k + 1, n ≠ 6, there exists an incomplete self-orthogonal latin square of order n with an empty order k subarray, called an ISOLS(n;k), except perhaps when (n;k) ∈ {(6m + i;2m):i = 2, 6}.\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nSelf-orthogonal latin squares (SOLS)\n\nChapter\n\nEarly examples of spouse avoidance\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jan 2008\n\n  * Ian Anderson\n\nSpouse-avoiding mixed doubles round robin tournaments were defined in 1973 by Brayton, Coppersmith and Hoffman. We discuss some earlier appearances of these designs in the late nineteenth century.\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nBalanced equitable mixed doubles round-robin tournaments\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jan 2013\n\n  * David R. Berman\n  * Douglas D. Smith\n\nView\n\nSelf-orthogonal Latin squares\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jan 1976\n\n  * 【13†R. We propose an algorithm based on tabu search to tackle the problem. Our algorithm constructed 32 new cyclic PBIBD(2)s. © 2004 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.\n\n\nThe spectrum of HSOLSSOM(hn) where h is even\n\nArticle\n\n  * Oct 1996\n  * DISCRETE MATH\n\n  * Frank E. Bennett\n  * Lie Zhu\n\nIn this paper, we describe a generalized product construction and a construction using Steiner pentagon systems to obtain holey self-orthogonal Latin squares with symmetric orthogonal mates (HSOLSSOM). We investigate the existence of HSOLSSOM(hn) for even h. We first improve the known result for h = 2 and show that a HSOLSSOM(2n) exists for all n ⩾ 5 except possibly for n ∈ E = {8, 10, 12, 14, 15, 16, 18, 20, 22, 24, 28, 32}. As a consequence, we then establish that for h  2 (mod 4) and h ⩾ 10 a HSOLSSOM(hn) exists for all n ⩾ 5 except possibly for nϵE. More conclusively, we show that for h  0 (mod 4), a HSOLSSOM(hn) exists if and only if n ⩾ 5. We are also able to apply our results to construct a unipotent SOLSSOM(62), the existence of which was previously unknown.\n\nExistence of frame SOLS of type\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jan 2002\n\n  * Yunqing Xu\n  * Hantao Zhang\n  * Lie Zhu\n\nView\n\nORTHOGONAL LATIN SQUARES\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jul 1959\n  * P NATL ACAD SCI USA\n\n  * E. T. Parker\n\nView\n\n\n  * D R Berman\n  * D D Smith\n  * Mitchell Tournaments\n\nD.R. Berman and D.D. Smith, Mitchell tournaments, Bull. Inst. Combin. Appl. 65 (2012), 33-42.\n\nSome new HSOLSSOMs of type h n and 1 n u 1\n\n  * Jan 2001\n  * J COMB DES\n  * 435-444\n\n  * G Ge\n  * R J R Abel\n\nG. Ge and R.J.R. Abel, Some new HSOLSSOMs of type h n and 1 n u 1, J. Combin. Des. 9 (2001), 435-444.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBalanced Equitable Mixed Doubles Round-Robin (https://paperzz.com/doc/8919938/balanced-equitable-mixed-doubles-round-robin)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month;     the number of games in a schedule is given when a balanced equitable structure ...     Mitchell design is available, unless a spouse-avoiding tournament is preferred\n    are summarized in the following table. The entry “all” indicates that the\n    structure contains all possible partnerships or oppositions and “one-half”\n    indicates that each player in the (non-strict) MMDRR(n) described in Section 3\n    opposes half of all the opposite-sex players. On the existence side of the chart,\n    the number of games in a schedule is given when a balanced equitable structure\n    exists, and “X” indicates either that the structure does not exist for the given size\n    or that there is no BE schedule. The notation “(?)” indicates that, while the\n    structure exists with the indicated number of games, it is not known wheth